<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #carriers>>

!! A carrier naming no control code

A carrier opens on the sharktooth and the caret, and one carrying no recognised code still
opens a carrier:

<<^>>

<<^ from=lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/carriers>>

!! A lar URI carrying a query and a fragment

<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/handoff?key=value&other=second#/fragment>>

<<~ loulou lar://mara:operator@crossroads/operator.reads.the-record?a=1&b=2>>

!! Strings in each quoting the dialect takes

<<~ ranks name='a single-quoted value' other="a double-quoted value">>

<<~ ranks title=[[a bracketed value]] other='second'>>

<<~ ranks bare=unquoted spaced = "value after spaces">>

The colon spelling names a parameter too, and it takes a quoted or a bracketed value:

<<~ kue voice:Mischief-Muse key:"a floor recites the bearings instead of inhabiting them">>

<<~ hoike held:[[a bracketed value carrying spaces]] voice:'a single-quoted value'>>

The equals spelling takes a bracketed value too, whole rather than to its first space:

<<~ sigil bracket=[[a bracketed value]] single='a single quoted value' bare=unquoted>>

!! A stray angle bracket answers the same way it answers in wikitext

A dialect wrapping the wikitext grammar carries its injections or loses what they paint, so a
stray < angle bracket takes the verdict here that it takes there.

!! A lar URI carries a query, and a bearing arrow stands in prose

A query names its terms after the path — lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono?mode=draft&hud=aim — and a
bearing arrow -> reads as a bearing wherever the base grammar names nothing.

<<~/ahu>>

